              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Second-order arithmetic is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic"} arithmetic
            dealing with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            and, what makes it “second-order”,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            of natural numbers
            %sup#fnref:1>
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            \. SOA, or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Z
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z_2
            as it is often denoted, is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof-theoretically
            weak in comparison with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} language
            of SOA consists of two sorts, here denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P N
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A constant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                of type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %li
              %p
                An unary function symbol
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi N
                        %mo →
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \colon N \to N
            %li
              %p
                Binary function symbols
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi N
                        %mo ×
                        %mi N
                        %mo →
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +, \cdot \colon N \times N \to N
            %li
              %p
                A binary relation symbol
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &lt;
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lt
                of type
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo ×
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \times N
            %li
              %p
                A binary relation symbol
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                of type
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo ×
                        %mi P
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \times P N
          %p
            The axioms of SOA may be divided into two parts: the first part comprises the “first-order axioms” that deal only with the sort
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "." do
              %em Robinson arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comprehension+schemes"} comprehension schemes
            that involve the symbol
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
          %h3#firstorder_axioms “First-order” axioms
          %p
            Omitting the sort
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P N
            and the symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            from the language, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} axioms
            in this section give
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robinson+arithmetic"} Robinson arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
            (predicate) logic with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+predicate"} equality
          %p For all the formulas in this section, it is tacitly understood that there are universal quantifiers at the heads of formulas, binding all variables which appear freely.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(m) = 0 \rightarrow \bot
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi m
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(m) = s(n) \rightarrow m = n
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi m
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0 = n) \vee (\exists_m s(m) = n)
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m + 0 = m
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m + s(n) = s(m+n)
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \cdot 0 = 0
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \cdot s(n) = (m\cdot n)+m]
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \lt 0 \rightarrow \bot
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \lt s(n) \leftrightarrow ((m \lt n) \vee (m=n))
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0 = n) \vee (0 \lt n)
                \.
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↔
                      %mi m
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ((s(m) \lt n) \vee (s(m) = n)) \leftrightarrow m \lt n
                \.
          %h3#secondorder_axioms “Second-order” axioms
          %p
            Note: according to convention, lower-case letters refer to terms of type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P N
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comprehension+scheme"}> Comprehension scheme
                \: for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} formula
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
                in the language of SOA with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variables"} free variables
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{m} = m_1, \ldots, m_j
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{X} = X_1, \ldots, X_k
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mover
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo ∀
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ↔
                      %mi φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \vec{m} \forall \vec{X} \exists Z \forall n (n\in Z \leftrightarrow \varphi(n))
              %p
                provided that the variable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                does not appear in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi φ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alpha-equivalence"} alpha-equivalence
                to rename variables, it's enough that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                does not appear free in
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi φ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            %li
              %p
                Full
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
                scheme: for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
                any formula with a free variable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                and possible remaining free variables
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{m} = m_1, \ldots, m_j
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi …
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{X} = X_1, \ldots, X_k
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mover
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ∀
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi n
                  %mi φ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\forall \vec{m} \forall \vec{X} ((\varphi(0) \wedge \forall n (\varphi(n) \rightarrow \varphi(s(n))) \rightarrow \forall n \varphi(n))
          %p
            The instance in the full induction scheme where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            is the formula
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in X
            is called simply the
            = succeed "." do
              %em induction axiom
          %h2#subsystems_of_soa Subsystems of SOA
          %p
            The theory described above gives full second-order arithmetic. However, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reverse+mathematics"} reverse mathematics
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reverse_mathematics"} Wikipedia
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Sim"} Simpson
          %p
            Many important subsystems for SOA have been the subject of an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+analysis"} ordinal analysis
          %p
            Another important subsystem is
            %em intuitionistic
            second-order arithmetic, or second-order
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+arithmetic"} Heyting arithmetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            rather than classical logic as the underlying first-order logic. In a subsystem without the induction axiom, we must add axioms stating that equality and order are
            = succeed ":" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                decidable
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/decidable+predicate"} ?
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (m = n) \vee ((m = n) \rightarrow \bot)
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (m \lt n) \vee ((m \lt n) \rightarrow \bot)
                \.
          %p Full (classical) SOA may be recovered from intuitionistic SOA by adding a similar axiom that set membership is decidable; in a subsystem without the comprehension scheme, this needs to be strengthened to an axiom scheme that every proposition (with any free variables) is true or false (effectively making the logic classical again).
